\title{Monotone Decline Versus Interior Modes:\\ A Shape/Leakage Decomposition for Positional Rate Profiles}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete mathematical decomposition of the inference problem that arises when a positional rate profile is tested for shape against a flexible alternative. Three phenomena are separated and settled as theorems. First, \emph{nonlinearity does not imply a mode}: the scale-free family $T(x) = C(1+x)^{-a}$ with $C,a>0$ has a strictly convex log-rate --- so no affine model can fit it and a likelihood-ratio test against a linear-in-$x$ model must reject --- while being strictly decreasing and therefore provably free of interior maxima on every window. Second, \emph{an endpoint-calibrated residual against a curvature-mismatched baseline manufactures a mid-window peak}: for a baseline $B(x) = C'(1+x)^{-a'}e^{-bx}$ the log-residual reduces to $d\log(1+x)+bx$ with mismatch $d = a'-a$, and if $d>0$ with the tilt calibrated to match the residual at the window edges, the residual acquires a strict interior maximum at $x^\star = L(1+l,1+u)-1$, where $L$ is the logarithmic mean. We prove that this artefact is trapped strictly between the geometric mean of the window edges and the window midpoint (hence lies in the left half of the window), that its amplitude is exactly proportional to $d$ while its location is independent of $d$, that the sign of $d$ decides bump versus no bump, and that the phenomenon persists for an arbitrary strictly convex log-baseline excess; conversely a strict interior mode is impossible when the log-baseline differs from the log-signal by an affine function, so an observed strict interior mode certifies baseline misspecification. Third, \emph{binning is innocent}: equal-width block averages of a continuous strictly decreasing shape are strictly decreasing for every bin width. We apply the decomposition to a binning-free re-analysis of a positional hit-rate experiment ($128$ strata, $9\,594$ hits against $512\,000$ controls) in which a free spline defeated the linear model with a likelihood-ratio statistic of $100.574$ on $3$ degrees of freedom ($p_{\mathrm{asym}} = 1.17\times10^{-21}$; $0/400$ permutation exceedances) while the profile's interior maximum was pinned to the extreme left edge in all $150$ bootstrap replicates. The correct reading is a steep monotone decline with peak-to-end ratio $2.54$ (CI $[2.243,2.798]$), not a positional mode; a previously reported peaked mid-window residual is retracted as baseline-curvature leakage, and the power-law description $T(x)\approx 0.0295\,(1+x)^{-1.104}$ is re-confirmed binning-free.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} power-law rate profile, interior mode, logarithmic mean, baseline misspecification, curvature leakage, likelihood-ratio test, binning invariance, Dickman-type decline.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The inferential situation}

A recurring pattern in empirical work is the \emph{positional shape test}. Events occur at positions $x$ inside a window $[l,u]$; one asks whether the event rate $T(x)$ has a preferred interior location --- a \textbf{mode} --- or whether it merely declines. The distinction is decisive: a monotone decline is generic, whereas an interior maximum localises a mechanism at a specific position.

In practice the question is almost never asked directly. Instead one fits a flexible model and tests it against a restrictive null, most often the model that is \emph{linear in $x$} on the log-rate scale. A large likelihood-ratio statistic is then reported as evidence of ``real structure''. The step from ``real structure'' to ``there is a peak'' is the fallacy this paper closes.

A second and subtler failure mode arises because absolute rates are rarely observable. What is reported is a \textbf{residual}: signal over baseline, on the log scale, tilted so as to match at the window edges. We show that this construction has an intrinsic tendency to produce interior bumps whenever the baseline's curvature differs from the signal's, and we characterise the resulting artefact exactly.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Shape calculus} (\S3): uniqueness of a strict interior mode; impossibility of interior modes for strictly antitone, strictly convex, and affine shapes.
\item \textbf{Nonlinearity without mode} (\S4): the power law is non-affine on the log scale and mode-free; explicit identification of the exponent from a peak-to-end ratio, with a steepness criterion separating $a<1$ from $a>1$.
\item \textbf{Curvature leakage} (\S5): existence, exact location, sign dichotomy, and a model-free generalisation of the manufactured mid-window mode; plus the converse erratum principle.
\item \textbf{Location and amplitude bounds} (\S6): GM $<$ LM $<$ AM in a self-contained hyperbolic normal form, yielding the left-half trap and the right-half falsifier, and the exact linear amplitude law.
\item \textbf{Binning invariance} (\S7): block averages of a strictly declining continuous shape are strictly declining.
\item \textbf{Application} (\S8): the decomposition applied to the experimental record, producing an erratum and a strengthened power-law conclusion.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Notation and standing conventions}

Throughout, $l<u$ are real with $-1<l$, and $[l,u]$ is the \textbf{window}. Position is measured in a shifted coordinate:
\[ A := 1+l, \qquad B := 1+u, \qquad 0<A<B, \qquad \rho := B/A \ \ (\text{the \emph{window ratio}}). \]
All logarithms are natural.

\begin{definition}[Interior mode]
A point $x$ is an \emph{interior mode} of $f$ on $[l,u]$ if $x\in(l,u)$ and $f(y)\le f(x)$ for all $y\in[l,u]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Strict interior mode]
A point $x$ is a \emph{strict interior mode} of $f$ on $[l,u]$ if $x\in(l,u)$ and $f(y)<f(x)$ for every $y\in[l,u]$ with $y\neq x$.
\end{definition}

A strict interior mode is in particular an interior mode, and both notions depend only on the restriction of $f$ to the window.

\begin{definition}[Power-law rate]
For $C,a\in\mathbb{R}$ and $x>-1$, $T_{C,a}(x) := C(1+x)^{-a}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Mixture-proxy baseline]
For $C',a',b\in\mathbb{R}$, $B_{C',a',b}(x) := C'(1+x)^{-a'}e^{-bx}$. This exponential-times-power form is the analytically tractable proxy for a mixture-Dickman baseline: it is closed under the log-residual operation, and its two curvature parameters isolate the scale-free curvature and the linear tilt.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Log-residual]
For $d,b\in\mathbb{R}$ and $x>-1$, $r_{d,b}(x) := d\log(1+x)+bx$. Here $d$ is the \textbf{curvature mismatch} and $b$ the \textbf{tilt}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Logarithmic mean]
For $0<A<B$, $\displaystyle L(A,B) := \frac{B-A}{\log B-\log A}$.
\end{definition}

\section{A calculus of interior modes}

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness]\label{lem:uniq}
If $x$ and $x'$ are both strict interior modes of $f$ on $[l,u]$, then $x=x'$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $x\ne x'$, strictness at $x$ gives $f(x')<f(x)$ and strictness at $x'$ gives $f(x)<f(x')$: a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Monotone decline has no interior mode]\label{lem:anti}
If $l<u$ and $f$ is strictly antitone on $[l,u]$, then no $x$ is an interior mode of $f$ on $[l,u]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $x\in(l,u)$. Then $l<x$ with $l\in[l,u]$, so $f(x)<f(l)$, violating $f(l)\le f(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Convexity forbids interior modes]\label{lem:conv}
Let $l<u$. If $f$ is strictly convex on $[l,u]$, no $x$ is an interior mode. If $f$ is convex, no $x$ is a strict interior mode.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
An interior $x$ equals $(1-t)l+tu$ with $t\in(0,1)$. Strict convexity gives $f(x)<(1-t)f(l)+tf(u)\le\max\{f(l),f(u)\}$, so $x$ fails to dominate one endpoint. In the convex case the same computation yields $f(x)\le\max\{f(l),f(u)\}$, contradicting strict domination of that endpoint.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Affine residuals are mode-free]\label{lem:affine}
For any $p,q\in\mathbb{R}$ and $l<u$, the map $y\mapsto p+qy$ has no strict interior mode on $[l,u]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Affine functions are convex; apply the second half of Lemma~\ref{lem:conv}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Log-curvature certificate]
Let $l<u$ and suppose $g=\log(T/B)$ is convex on $[l,u]$ for positive $T,B$. Then $T/B$ has no strict interior mode on $[l,u]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:conv}, $g$ has no strict interior mode; since $\exp$ is strictly increasing, $T/B=e^{g}$ has a strict interior mode exactly where $g$ does.
\end{proof}

\section{Nonlinearity without a mode}

\begin{proposition}[The power law is mode-free]\label{prop:modefree}
Let $C>0$, $a>0$. Then $T_{C,a}$ is strictly antitone on $(-1,\infty)$, has no interior mode on any window $[l,u]\subseteq(-1,\infty)$, and attains its maximum over the window only at the left edge $l$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $-1<y_1<y_2$ we have $0<1+y_1<1+y_2$ and $t\mapsto t^{-a}$ is strictly decreasing on $(0,\infty)$; multiply by $C>0$. Then apply Lemma~\ref{lem:anti}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Strict convexity of the log-rate]\label{prop:logconv}
For $a>0$, $\ell(x) := -a\log(1+x)$ is strictly convex on $(-1,\infty)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\log$ is strictly concave, so $-a\log$ is strictly convex for $a>0$, and composition with the affine map $x\mapsto1+x$ preserves strict convexity; equivalently $\ell''(x)=a/(1+x)^2>0$. The midpoint form $\log\!\big(\tfrac{A+B}{2}\big)>\tfrac12(\log A+\log B)$ for positive $A\ne B$ suffices below.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No affine log-fit]\label{thm:noaffine}
Let $C,a>0$ and $-1<l<u$. There are no $p,q\in\mathbb{R}$ with $\log T_{C,a}(x)=p+qx$ for all $x\in[l,u]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Such $p,q$ would force the strictly convex $\ell$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:logconv} to agree with an affine function on $[l,u]$. At the midpoint $m=(l+u)/2$, strict convexity gives $\ell(m)<\tfrac12(\ell(l)+\ell(u))$ while the affine representation gives equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonlinearity without mode --- headline decomposition]\label{thm:headline}
Let $C>0$, $a>0$, $-1<l<u$. Then simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}
\item no affine $p+qx$ agrees with $\log T_{C,a}$ on $[l,u]$;
\item $T_{C,a}$ is strictly antitone on $[l,u]$;
\item no point of $(l,u)$ is an interior mode of $T_{C,a}$ on $[l,u]$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:noaffine}, Proposition~\ref{prop:modefree}, Lemma~\ref{lem:anti}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
A likelihood-ratio test of ``free smooth log-rate'' against ``log-rate affine in $x$'' is guaranteed to reject asymptotically when the truth is any power law with $a\ne0$. The magnitude of the rejection is a statement about \emph{curvature}; item (3) says the truth may nevertheless have no interior maximum. Hence significance against linearity is not evidence for a mode. More generally, by Lemma~\ref{lem:conv} the entire convex-log class is structurally incapable of an interior maximum.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Ratio law]\label{prop:ratio}
For $C\ne0$ and $-1<l$, $-1<u$: $\ \dfrac{T_{C,a}(l)}{T_{C,a}(u)}=\Big(\dfrac{1+u}{1+l}\Big)^{a}=\rho^{a}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Exponent identification]\label{thm:expid}
If $-1<l<u$ and $R=T_{C,a}(l)/T_{C,a}(u)$, then $a=\log R/\log\rho$ with $\rho=(1+u)/(1+l)>1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take logarithms in Proposition~\ref{prop:ratio} and divide by $\log\rho>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Steepness test]\label{cor:steep}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:expid}, if $R>\rho$ then $a>1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Two-point identifiability]
If $T_{C,a}$ and $T_{C',a'}$ agree at two distinct points of $(-1,\infty)$ with $C,C'>0$, then $C=C'$ and $a=a'$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Taking logs and subtracting the two equations gives $(a'-a)\big(\log(1+x_1)-\log(1+x_2)\big)=0$; the second factor is nonzero by injectivity of $\log$, so $a=a'$ and then $C=C'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monotone ratio in the exponent]
For fixed $\rho>1$ the map $a\mapsto\rho^{a}$ is strictly increasing: the peak-to-end ratio is a monotone summary statistic for steepness.
\end{proposition}

\section{Curvature leakage: how a baseline manufactures a peak}

\begin{theorem}[Residual normal form]\label{thm:normal}
For $C,C'>0$ and $x>-1$,
\[ \log\frac{T_{C,a}(x)}{B_{C',a',b}(x)} = \log\frac{C}{C'} + r_{d,b}(x), \qquad d=a'-a. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $\log T = \log C - a\log(1+x)$ and $\log B = \log C' - a'\log(1+x) - bx$ and subtract.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Matching tilt]
$\displaystyle b^\star(d,l,u) := -\,d\,\frac{\log(1+u)-\log(1+l)}{u-l}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tilt}
If $d>0$ and $-1<l<u$ then $b^\star<0$; and for every $d$, $r_{d,b^\star}(l)=r_{d,b^\star}(u)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Negativity is immediate since $\log(1+u)>\log(1+l)$ and $u-l>0$. Endpoint equality: $r_{d,b^\star}(u)-r_{d,b^\star}(l) = d\big(\log(1+u)-\log(1+l)\big)+b^\star(u-l)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tangent-line maximality]\label{thm:tangent}
Let $d>0$ and $b<0$. Then $r_{d,b}(y)<r_{d,b}(x^\star)$ for every $y>-1$ with $y\ne x^\star$, where $x^\star=-d/b-1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $m:=-d/b>0$, so $1+x^\star=m$ and $b=-d/m$. For $y>-1$ let $t:=(1+y)/m>0$. The strict tangent inequality $\log t<t-1$ for $t\ne1$ gives
\[ d\big(\log(1+y)-\log m\big) < d\Big(\frac{1+y}{m}-1\Big) = -b\,(y-x^\star), \]
using $d/m=-b$. Rearranging yields $d\log(1+y)+by< d\log m+bx^\star=r_{d,b}(x^\star)$; the excluded case $t=1$ is precisely $y=x^\star$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The peak is the logarithmic mean]\label{thm:logmean}
For $d>0$ and $-1<l<u$,
\[ \frac{-d}{b^\star}-1 = L(1+l,1+u)-1 = \frac{u-l}{\log(1+u)-\log(1+l)}-1 . \]
In particular the location is independent of the mismatch $d$, which cancels from the stationarity equation $d/(1+x)+b^\star=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[The logarithmic mean is interior]\label{lem:Linterior}
For $0<A<B$, $L(A,B)\in(A,B)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Strict concavity of $\log$ gives $(B-A)/B<\log B-\log A<(B-A)/A$; dividing $B-A$ by these bounds yields $A<L<B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Baseline leakage manufactures a mid-window mode]\label{thm:ghost}
Let $d>0$ and $-1<l<u$. Then $x^\star=L(1+l,1+u)-1$ is a strict interior mode of $r_{d,b^\star}$ on $[l,u]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Interiority is Lemma~\ref{lem:Linterior} translated by $-1$; Lemma~\ref{lem:tilt} gives $b^\star<0$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:tangent} applies and its maximiser equals $x^\star$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:logmean}. Restrict to $[l,u]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The signal has no such mode]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:ghost} with $C,a>0$, the signal $T_{C,a}$ has no interior mode on $[l,u]$ while its endpoint-matched residual against a more-curved baseline does. The peak is entirely an artefact of the comparison.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Strict interior modes force non-affine log-residuals]\label{thm:erratum}
If $f$ has a strict interior mode on $[l,u]$ with $l<u$, then $f$ is not affine on $[l,u]$. Equivalently, if $\log T-\log B$ is affine on the window, no strict interior mode can be reported.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:affine}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The erratum principle]
Within the power-law family a positional mode cannot exist. Therefore an observed strict interior mode in a log-residual proves either that the signal leaves the power-law family, or that the log-baseline differs from the log-signal by a \emph{non-affine} function --- baseline misspecification. Since the shape channel tests the first hypothesis and finds monotone decline pinned at the left edge, the surviving explanation is the second.
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}[Matched-endpoint concave shapes have a mode]\label{lem:concmode}
Let $l<u$ and let $f$ be continuous and strictly concave on $[l,u]$ with $f(l)=f(u)$. Then $f$ has a strict interior mode on $[l,u]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Compactness gives a maximiser $x$. It is not an endpoint: for interior $y$, strict concavity gives $f(y)>\min\{f(l),f(u)\}=f(l)=f(u)$. Uniqueness of the maximiser follows since two distinct maximisers would make the midpoint value exceed the maximum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[General curvature leakage]\label{thm:general}
Let $g$ be continuous and strictly convex on $[l,u]$, $l<u$, and let $c:=(g(u)-g(l))/(u-l)$. Then $y\mapsto cy-g(y)$ has a strict interior mode on $[l,u]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$y\mapsto cy$ is concave and subtracting a strictly convex function yields a strictly concave function; its endpoint values agree by definition of $c$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:concmode}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Concavity/convexity dichotomy]\label{prop:dichot}
For $d>0$ and any $b$, $r_{d,b}$ is strictly concave on $(-1,\infty)$; for $d\le0$ and any $b$, it is convex, since $r_{d,b}''(x)=-d/(1+x)^2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Under-curved baselines never leak a peak]
If $d\le0$ then for \emph{every} tilt $b$ the residual $r_{d,b}$ has no strict interior mode on $[l,u]$. If $d<0$, the endpoint-matched residual has no interior mode at all.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Convexity (Proposition~\ref{prop:dichot}) with Lemma~\ref{lem:conv}. For $d<0$ the endpoint-matched residual is strictly convex, so its maximum sits at an endpoint.
\end{proof}

\section{Trapping the ghost: location bounds and amplitude law}

\begin{lemma}[Hyperbolic normal form]\label{lem:hyp}
For $0<A<B$ there is $s>0$ with $B=Ae^{2s}$ and $\log B-\log A=2s$, and then
\[ \sqrt{AB}=Ae^{s}, \qquad L(A,B)=Ae^{s}\frac{\sinh s}{s}, \qquad \frac{A+B}{2}=Ae^{s}\cosh s. \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Take $s=(\log B-\log A)/2$. Then $B-A=A(e^{2s}-1)=2Ae^{s}\sinh s$ and $(A+B)/2=A(1+e^{2s})/2=Ae^{s}\cosh s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tanh}
For $s>0$, $\sinh s<s\cosh s$ (equivalently $\tanh s<s$).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $\varphi(t)=t\cosh t-\sinh t$; then $\varphi'(t)=t\sinh t>0$ for $t>0$, so $\varphi$ is strictly increasing on $[0,\infty)$ and $\varphi(0)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sinh}
For $s>0$, $s<\sinh s$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Mean inequalities]\label{thm:means}
For $0<A<B$: $\ \sqrt{AB}<L(A,B)<\dfrac{A+B}{2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:hyp} the three quantities are $Ae^{s}$ times $1$, $\sinh(s)/s$ and $\cosh s$; apply Lemmas~\ref{lem:sinh} and \ref{lem:tanh}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ghost trapped between geometric mean and midpoint]\label{thm:trap}
Let $d>0$ and $-1<l<u$. The strict interior mode $x^\star=L(1+l,1+u)-1$ satisfies
\[ \sqrt{(1+l)(1+u)}-1 \;<\; x^\star \;<\; \frac{l+u}{2}. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:means} with $A=1+l$, $B=1+u$, subtracting $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Right-half falsifier]\label{thm:falsifier}
Let $d>0$, $-1<l<u$ and $x\ge(l+u)/2$. Then $x$ is not a strict interior mode of $r_{d,b^\star}$ on $[l,u]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:ghost} the residual has a strict interior mode at $x^\star$, and strict interior modes are unique (Lemma~\ref{lem:uniq}); so $x=x^\star<(l+u)/2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:trap}, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Quantitatively, the ghost's position as a fraction of the window is
\[ \frac{x^\star-l}{u-l} = \frac{L(A,B)-A}{B-A} = \frac12\left(\frac1s-\frac{e^{-s}}{\sinh s}\right)\in\Big(0,\tfrac12\Big), \qquad s=\tfrac12\log\rho, \]
tending to $1/2$ as $\rho\to1$ (narrow windows: the ghost approaches the midpoint) and to $0$ as $\rho\to\infty$ (wide windows: the ghost crowds the left edge). On the unit window $[0,1]$ one gets $x^\star=1/\log2-1=0.442695\ldots$, trapped between $\sqrt2-1=0.414214$ and $0.5$.

\begin{definition}[Ghost amplitude]
$\Amp(d,l,u) := r_{d,b^\star}\big(L(A,B)-1\big)-r_{d,b^\star}(l)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Amplitude law]\label{thm:amp}
For all $d,l,u$ one has $\Amp(d,l,u)=d\,\Amp(1,l,u)$, and for $d>0$, $-1<l<u$,
\[ \Amp(d,l,u) = d\left[\log\frac{L}{A}-1+\frac{A}{L}\right] > 0, \qquad A=1+l,\ L=L(A,B). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Homogeneity: $b^\star(d,l,u)=d\,b^\star(1,l,u)$ and $r_{d,db}(x)=d\,r_{1,b}(x)$, while the location $L-1$ does not involve $d$. Positivity follows from strict domination of the endpoint in Theorem~\ref{thm:ghost}. Closed form: $b^\star(1,l,u)=-1/L$, so $r_{1,-1/L}(x)=\log(1+x)-x/L$; evaluating at $x=L-1$ and $x=l$ and subtracting gives $\log(L/A)-(L-A)/L$, positive because $\log t>1-1/t$ for $t=L/A>1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Experimental signature]
Doubling the curvature mismatch exactly doubles the height of a leakage bump and leaves its location unchanged. A candidate peak that scales linearly with an injected baseline curvature error without moving is leakage; one that moves, or fails to scale, is not. Inverting the amplitude law calibrates the mismatch: $d=\Amp\big/\big[\log(L/A)-1+A/L\big]$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Binning invariance: coarse-graining cannot create a peak}

\begin{theorem}[Sliding a window right lowers the integral]\label{thm:slide}
Let $h>0$, $s<t$, and let $f$ be continuous and strictly antitone on $[s,t+h]$. Then
$\int_{t}^{t+h}f<\int_{s}^{s+h}f$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both integrals equal $\int_0^h f(t+\xi)\,d\xi$ and $\int_0^h f(s+\xi)\,d\xi$. For each $\xi\in[0,h]$, $s+\xi<t+\xi$ lie in $[s,t+h]$, so $f(t+\xi)\le f(s+\xi)$, with strict inequality at $\xi=0$; continuity then makes the integral inequality strict.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Block mean]
$\displaystyle \overline{f}_k := \frac1h\int_{l+kh}^{l+kh+h} f(x)\,dx$ for $k\in\mathbb{N}$, $h>0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Block averages of a declining power law decline]\label{thm:bin}
Let $C,a>0$, $-1<l$, $h>0$. Then $k\mapsto\overline{(T_{C,a})}_k$ is strictly decreasing.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $j<k$ put $s=l+jh$, $t=l+kh$; on $[s,t+h]\subseteq(-1,\infty)$ the power law is continuous and strictly antitone (Proposition~\ref{prop:modefree}); apply Theorem~\ref{thm:slide} and divide by $h>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The first bin strictly dominates every later bin, and no bin other than the first can be a maximum: the discrete analogue of Proposition~\ref{prop:modefree}. The same argument holds for any continuous strictly antitone shape --- binning can blur or attenuate a peak, never manufacture one.
\end{corollary}

\section{Application: the absolute-shape channel}

\subsection{Design}
The re-analysis used no binning. The data comprise $128$ strata with $9\,594$ hits against $512\,000$ controls, over windows of fixed ratio $3$. A stratum-conditional case--control logistic model was fitted to raw hit indicators with $128$ profiled intercepts, comparing a null with log-odds affine in position against a free natural cubic spline with $5$ degrees of freedom (knots at the $0.25/0.5/0.75$ quantiles). Calibration used within-stratum label permutations ($B=400$, on a design capped at $200$ controls per stratum) and a bootstrap with $150$ replicates; observed statistics came from the full design ($521\,594$ observation rows, $1\,024\,000$ control rows).

