By Theorem~\ref{thm:headline} the first rows and the pinned maximum are perfectly consistent: the power law is nonlinear \emph{and} mode-free. The registered location criterion (an interior maximum in $[0.4,0.8]$ of the window) fails, with zero-width confidence interval across all replicates.

By Theorem~\ref{thm:expid} the ratio $R=2.54$ identifies the exponent as $a=\log R/\log\rho$ in the shifted coordinate. The recorded description $T(x)\approx0.0295(1+x)^{-1.104}$ corresponds to an effective window ratio $\rho=R^{1/a}\approx2.327$; the interval $[2.243,2.798]$ on $R$ transfers to $a\in[0.957,1.219]$ at that $\rho$, bracketing the Dickman-type regime near $a\approx1$. Corollary~\ref{cor:steep} supplies the boundary test: $R>\rho$ if and only if the decline is steeper than reciprocal.

\subsection{The erratum}
A previously recorded finding reported a peaked mid-window residual. It is retracted and reclassified as \textbf{baseline-curvature leakage}: the residual was formed against a mixture-Dickman baseline and calibrated at the window edges, exactly the configuration of Lemma~\ref{lem:tilt}; by Theorem~\ref{thm:ghost} that configuration must display a strict interior maximum whenever the baseline's effective log-curvature exponent exceeds the signal's; by Theorem~\ref{thm:erratum} an observed strict interior mode certifies baseline misspecification rather than positional structure; and by Theorem~\ref{thm:bin} the alternative scapegoat, binning, is unavailable.

The power-law headline therefore stands and is strengthened, re-found binning-free with a strictly monotone decile profile and a null control arm. The absolute-shape channel closes. Any revival must be framed as a baseline-misspecification claim and must pass two independent tests: the peak must lie in the left half of the window, between the geometric mean of the edges and the midpoint (Theorem~\ref{thm:trap}; a right-half peak is not explicable by endpoint-matched leakage, Theorem~\ref{thm:falsifier}); and deliberately perturbing the baseline curvature must scale the peak height exactly linearly without moving it (Theorem~\ref{thm:amp}).

\subsection{Disclosures}
The comparison as executed used two legs: free spline versus linear, with permutation calibration. A monotone $I$-spline / isotonic leg and an exact Dickman-offset leg were not run, so the ``beats monotone'' clause of the alternative remains untested; this does not affect the conclusion because the location clause of the registered criterion fails independently. The permutation floor at $B=400$ is $p\le0.0025$, above a registered $p<0.001$ threshold unattainable at that $B$. Permutation and bootstrap legs used capped designs while observed statistics came from the full design. Total wall time $273.4$~s.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Shape-channel verdict.} Locate the argmax of the fitted profile on the window; bootstrap it; if the argmax mass concentrates at or adjacent to an edge, report \emph{no interior mode}, regardless of the linearity test's $p$-value; report the linearity likelihood ratio as a curvature statement only. Complexity $O(RN)$ for $R$ replicates and $N$ observations.

\paragraph{B. Ghost locator.} From the window alone compute $A=1+l$, $B=1+u$, $L=(B-A)/(\log B-\log A)$, and return $x^\star=L-1$ with the trap $[\sqrt{AB}-1,(l+u)/2]$. Complexity $O(1)$. Because the prediction precedes inspection of the residual, it is a genuine falsifier.

\paragraph{C. Amplitude-scaling test.} Inject a controlled curvature perturbation $\delta$ into the baseline exponent, recompute the endpoint-matched residual, and regress bump height on $\delta$. Under leakage the regression is exactly linear through the origin with slope $\log(L/A)-1+A/L$ and the fitted location is constant in $\delta$. Complexity $O(K)$ for $K$ perturbation levels.

\section{Discussion}

The three theorems are not deep; they are load-bearing. Each closes an inferential gap previously bridged by intuition: ``the $p$-value is astronomically small, so there must be a peak'' (closed by Theorem~\ref{thm:headline}); ``the residual bumps in the middle, so something happens in the middle'' (closed by Theorems~\ref{thm:ghost}, \ref{thm:trap}, \ref{thm:erratum}); ``maybe the bins did it'' (closed by Theorem~\ref{thm:bin}). The payoff is a clean closure that prevents both a wrong positional-mode hunt and a wrong retraction of a true power law.

It is striking that the artefact's location is a classical mean. The logarithmic mean appears in heat-exchanger design, in the theory of divided differences, and as the Stolarsky-family member interpolating geometric and arithmetic means. Its appearance here is not a coincidence: the stationarity condition of $d\log(1+x)+bx$ is $1+x=-d/b$, and endpoint matching makes $-d/b$ literally a divided difference over the window. The universality of the location --- its independence from $d$ --- is the statement that a divided difference does not know the size of the mismatch.

Limitations. The location and amplitude results are proved for the exponential-times-power baseline proxy; Theorem~\ref{thm:general} makes \emph{existence} fully general for strictly convex log-excess, but pinning the location at the logarithmic mean uses the specific $\log(1+x)$ curvature. Endpoint matching is essential: other calibration conventions will move the artefact somewhat and should be re-analysed individually. The results are deterministic shape statements, constraining point estimates rather than sampling distributions. Binning invariance is proved for equal-width blocks.

\section{Future directions}

Two sharp, falsifiable quantitative predictions accompany the three settled steps: the leakage ghost is trapped strictly between the geometric mean of the window edges and the window midpoint, and its amplitude is exactly proportional to the curvature mismatch while its location is independent of it. Open items: a rate-layer covariate for stratum size; a monotone $I$-spline / isotonic leg; and an exact Dickman-offset baseline (we used the analytically tractable exponential-times-power mixture as the proxy).

\emph{Direction 1 --- Log-mean pinning of curvature-leakage artefacts.} The endpoint-matched tilt cancels $d$ from the stationarity equation, leaving the logarithmic mean, a universal location depending only on the window. The two-sided trap is proved, as is existence for arbitrary strictly convex curvature excess; what remains is to show that the location pinning survives for an arbitrary smooth curvature excess rather than a pure exponent shift, turning ``peak in the right half'' into a decisive, model-free falsifier for any leakage explanation.

\emph{Direction 2 --- Curvature-signed dichotomy for residual shapes.} The sign of the second-derivative mismatch, not its size, decides bump versus dip. A full dichotomy theorem would classify first-order residual pathologies and give a sign test on residual curvature as a one-line misspecification diagnostic.

\emph{Direction 3 --- Rate-layer covariates.} With the shape layer closed, a covariate model at the rate layer can be estimated without contaminating the shape conclusion; the two layers are now formally separated.

\emph{Direction 4 --- Calibration-convention robustness.} Repeat the location analysis for least-squares-tilted, mean-matched and quantile-matched residuals; each convention should produce its own universal location constant, yielding a small atlas of ghosts usable as a lookup table.

\section{Conclusion}

A likelihood-ratio statistic of $100.574$ on $3$ degrees of freedom, with zero permutation exceedances in $400$ relabelings, establishes that a positional rate profile is not linear. It establishes nothing about a mode, because the fitted alternative --- a power law $T(x)=C(1+x)^{-a}$ --- is nonlinear and mode-free at once. The observed maximum sits at the left edge in every bootstrap replicate, the deciles decline strictly, and the peak-to-end ratio of $2.54$ measures a steep Dickman-type decline. The mid-window bump that had been reported is a ghost with a precise address: an endpoint-matched residual against a more-curved baseline must peak at the logarithmic mean of the window edges, strictly between their geometric mean and their midpoint, with height exactly proportional to the curvature mismatch and position entirely independent of it. That is not a hedge; it is a prediction, and it can be tested.

